{
  "raw": {
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601322": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies",
      "description": "Job Description - Business Analyst  (2601322) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Business Analyst  (2601322) Business Analyst - 2601322 __________ DIGITAL SERVICES DIVISION | RATONGA MATIHIKO The University of Otago is transforming how we support our people. Our People Pathway (OPP) is a major programme delivering a new Human Resources Information Management (Workday) system across the University. We are seeking a Business Analyst to support delivery of this high-impact programme. The role | Te mahi This role sits within Our People Pathway, the University's Workday implementation programme, supporting the delivery of new people, process, data and technology capability across the University. Working across business and technical teams, you will analyse requirements, map processes and help translate business needs into practical Workday solutions. This is an opportunity to contribute to a significant enterprise transformation that will improve how staff experience core HR services. Work will include: •    Eliciting, analysing and documenting business requirements for Workday processes, integrations, data, reporting and user experience. •    Mapping current and future state processes and identifying gaps, risks, impacts and opportunities for improvement. •    Facilitating workshops and discovery sessions with HR, operational, technical and vendor stakeholders. •    Supporting configuration, testing, data validation, reporting and change readiness activities by providing clear analysis and traceable requirements. •    Working collaboratively across project workstreams to ensure business needs are understood, prioritised and reflected in Workday delivery outcomes. •    Maintaining alignment with Workday functionality, implementation partner guidance and the University’s programme objectives. Skills and Experience | Kā Pūkeka Me Kā Wheako We are looking for a Business Analyst who can bring structure and clarity to complex work, translate business needs into clear requirements, and work effectively with a wide range of stakeholders. To succeed in this role, you will bring: •    A relevant qualification, certification, or equivalent experience in business analysis, project delivery, HR systems or digital transformation. •    Proven experience eliciting, analysing and documenting requirements and processes, with strong facilitation and stakeholder engagement skills. •    A practical understanding of project delivery methods, including agile, hybrid or implementation-based environments. •    The ability to connect process, data, system and user experience considerations to support quality outcomes. •    Experience with Workday, HRIS, ERP or other enterprise system implementations would be an advantage. About our Team | Mō Te Tīma Transformation and Improvement support the University’s success through strong programme delivery and business process improvement. This multi-year programme spans HR, Digital, Finance and the wider University, offering the opportunity to contribute to a complex, high-profile transformation. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), fixed term (2 years), based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin, with the possibility of extension. The appointments range for this role is $85,057 to $92,174. Top of range $97,508. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. We would consider a secondment arrangement where a current University employee is released to take up this position and then returns to their permanent position when this position ends. Any secondment arrangement would need the support of your current manager. If you would like to apply subject to a secondment arrangement being agreed, and have your manager’s support, please include this information in your covering letter. If you would like any additional information about secondments, please contact your Divisional HR Manager. For further information regarding the role please contact Luke Erickson, via the below contact details. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601322 will close on Thursday, 17 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Luke Erickson Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Digital Specialist Band C (Range of Appointments $85,057 to $92,174, Top of Range $97,508) Organisation Information Systems Job Function ICT Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601344": {
      "title": "Job Description -  Workday Specialist (2601344)",
      "description": "Job Description -  Workday Specialist (2601344) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description -  Workday Specialist (2601344) Workday Specialist - 2601344 __________ OFFICE OF TRANSFORMATION AND IMPROVEMENT | TE TARI WHAKAUMU ME TE WHAKAPAI AKE OPERATIONS DIVISION | TE WĀHANGA WHAKAHAERE The University of Otago is transforming how we support our people. Our People Pathway (OPP) is a major programme delivering a new Human Resources Information Management (Workday) system across the University. We are seeking a Workday Specialist to support delivery of this high-impact programme. The role | Te mahi This role sits within Our People Pathway, the University's Workday implementation programme, supporting the delivery of new people, process, data and technology capability across the University. Working across business and technical teams, you will provide functional Workday expertise, support solution design, and help translate business needs into practical Workday solutions. This is an opportunity to contribute to a significant enterprise transformation that will improve how staff experience core HR services. Work will include: •    Leading functional design across Workday modules and associated business processes. •    Facilitating workshops to define future-state processes, capture requirements and support decision-making. •    Translating business requirements into Workday functional designs and configuration documentation. •    Reviewing and validating configuration and workflows to ensure solutions meet business needs and align with programme objectives. •    Providing expert advice on Workday capabilities, best practice and process improvement opportunities. •    Supporting data migration, testing and quality assurance activities across the implementation lifecycle. •    Working collaboratively with stakeholders, Digital Services and the implementation partner to support delivery, change readiness and transition to business-as-usual operations. Skills and Experience | Kā Pūkeka Me Kā Wheako We are looking for a Workday Specialist who can bring expertise and practical thinking to complex work and work effectively with a wide range of stakeholders. To succeed in this role, you will bring: •    Proven experience delivering Workday implementations, enterprise system projects or digital transformation initiatives. •    Strong functional knowledge of one or more Workday modules and associated business processes. •    Experience facilitating workshops and supporting solution design with business and technical stakeholders. •    Experience supporting testing, issue resolution and quality assurance within a project or implementation environment. •    Strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to explain complex concepts clearly and influence decision-making. •    Workday certification and experience working in large, complex organisations would be an advantage. About our Team | Mō Te Tīma Transformation and Improvement support the University’s success through strong programme delivery and business process improvement. This multi-year programme spans HR, Digital, Finance and the wider University, offering the opportunity to contribute to a complex, high-profile transformation. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), fixed-term position for two years based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $96,587 to $105,532 per annum. Top of the range is $114,474. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information regarding the role please contact Stela Pasic, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601344 will close on Sunday, 20 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Stela Pasic Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Management Band 1 (Range of Appointments $96,587 to $105,532, Top of Range $114,474) Organisation Office of Transformation and Improvement Job Function Systems Architecture and Administration ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601330": {
      "title": "Job Description - Facilities Coordinator - Christchurch (2601330)",
      "description": "Job Description - Facilities Coordinator - Christchurch (2601330) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Facilities Coordinator - Christchurch (2601330) Facilities Coordinator - Christchurch - 2601330 __________ PROPERTY & CAMPUS DEVELOPMENT DIVISION KĀ RAWA ME TE WHANAKEHAKA PAPA TAURIMA The role / Te mahi Due to the launch of a new facility, this is a rare opportunity to join the University of Otago’s Christchurch campus as a Facilities Coordinator where you will be part of a team responsible for ensuring a safe, vibrant, and high-performing built environment is maintained to the highest standards. Reporting to the Facility Manager, you will coordinate a building portfolio including very modern, highly serviced and complex laboratory and research facilities. As a Facilities Coordinator, you will be an important member of the wider Property and Campus Development team and: •    Provide a key Property Operations point of contact for University staff and students. •    Proactively partner with your portfolio to fully understand their current and future requirements regarding the built environment and user needs. •    Anticipate and respond to the needs of a diverse range of facilities and operations. •    Ensure that daily work requests are promptly triaged, scoped and completed within service framework timeframes. •    Coordinate the planned and corrective maintenance of University buildings at their optimum levels and regulatory requirements. •    Ensure that all works carried out meet the University’s quality and design standards. In addition to the Christchurch based Facilities Manager, you will be supported by an array of property professionals in our Dunedin Campus, such as engineers, asset, project, property, and compliance managers as well as our dedicated Trade Services Team. Your skills and experience / Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful applicant will ideally have: •    Relevant Vocational, Facilities Management, Building Services Qualification, or experience (direct or transferable) in Property Operations, Project, Construction or Facilities Management with a strong client focus. •    Experience operating and maintaining commercial, teaching or research buildings. •    A sound understanding of the Building Act and the Health and Safety Act. •    A clear and proven understanding of as-builts, floor plans and operating and maintenance manuals. •    Proficient in the use of facility or asset management software and the Microsoft office suite of applications. •    Strong organisational and planning skills. While experience in trades disciplines, scientific, research, or medical facilities is highly beneficial to the role, dedication, curiosity, and attention to detail are highly valued for this role. About our team / Mō te tīma The Property & Campus Development Division is responsible for the University’s $2 billion built environment, supporting over 4,000 staff and 20,000 students across multiple campuses. When staff, students, and the public step onto our campuses, they experience a range of learning and research environments, including classrooms, world leading laboratories and libraries. The dedicated efforts of the Property and Campus Development Division team shape these experiences. We're the physical foundation of the teaching, research, learning and social interaction that takes place at the University, and we're proud of the important role we play. Further details / Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours), 2-year fixed term position, located in Christchurch. We offer competitive salary, five weeks’ annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. More information regarding employee benefits can be found here . You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this vacancy. The appointment range for this role is $69,931 to $74,609. Top of the range is $80,285, per annum. Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka | University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. We welcome the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence – please contact Rob Wilks, via the contact details below. Application / Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter), please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601330 will close on Sunday, 27 September 2026. Applications will be reviewed as submitted and candidates may be invited to interview immediately. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Rob Wilks Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Christchurch Primary Location NZL-SI-Christchurch Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Level 5 (Range of Appointments $69,931 to $74,609, Top of Range $80,285) Organisation Property Operations Job Function General Services ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601348": {
      "title": "Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow - Microbiology (2601348)",
      "description": "Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow - Microbiology (2601348) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow - Microbiology (2601348) Postdoctoral Fellow - Microbiology - 2601348 __________ DEPARTMENT OF MICROBIOLOGY & IMMUNOLOGY | TE TARI MOROMOROITI ME TE ĀRAI MATE HEALTH SCIENCES DIVISION | TE TAUTARINUI We are seeking a highly motivated Postdoctoral Fellow with strong experimental microbiology expertise to join the Pletzer laboratory in the Department of Microbiology and Immunology and work closely with the Gamble laboratory in the School of Pharmacy at the University of Otago, located in beautiful Ōtepoti / Dunedin, New Zealand. Mō mātou | About us The Pletzer Laboratory investigates antimicrobial resistance, bacterial pathogenesis, and new approaches to treating infectious diseases using complementary in vitro and in vivo infection models. The Gamble Laboratory has expertise in synthetic, bioorganic, and medicinal chemistry, with a particular focus on developing new therapeutic approaches. Together, the groups provide a highly multidisciplinary research environment spanning microbiology, medicinal and synthetic chemistry, and antimicrobial drug development. The successful candidate will work closely with researchers across both laboratories and the wider project team. Te mahi | The role This position is part of the Wellcome Leap Focused Antibiotics programme aiming to transform how existing antibiotics are delivered so that infections can be effectively treated while minimizing disruption of the gut microbiome. The University of Otago is one of only 16 teams selected internationally for the programme. The Otago project focuses on developing antibiotic strategies that are delivered to the site of infection, retaining antibacterial efficacy while minimising exposure of the gut microbiome. The successful candidate will lead the experimental microbiology and infection-model components of the project. The work will include evaluating antibiotic activity against clinically relevant bacterial pathogens, optimizing in vitro assays, and assessing lead compounds in preclinical infection models. A major focus will be developing and applying in vivo models of bacterial infection to determine antibacterial efficacy and support progression of promising candidates. The project is highly collaborative and milestone-driven, providing an opportunity to work with researchers at Otago and with internationally recognized teams in antibiotic development, drug delivery and microbiome research participating in the wider Wellcome Leap program. Kā pūkeka me kā wheako | Your skills and experience Applicants must hold a PhD in microbiology, infection biology, antimicrobial research or a closely related discipline. The successful candidate will have: •    substantial hands-on experience in experimental bacteriology and microbiology, including bacterial culture, quantitative microbiology and antimicrobial susceptibility testing; •    demonstrated ability to design, optimise and independently execute microbiological experiments; •    experience analysing, interpreting and communicating complex experimental datasets; •    a strong research track record, including first-author publications in reputable peer-reviewed journals; •    excellent organisational skills and the ability to work independently within a fast-moving, milestone-driven research programme; •    strong written and verbal communication skills and the ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary team. Experience with in vivo bacterial infection models is strongly preferred. Candidates with demonstrated experience working with mouse models of bacterial infection will be particularly competitive. Experience with various routes of infection, therapeutic intervention studies, quantitative bacterial burden measurements, tissue collection and processing, and/or longitudinal infection imaging is a plus. Additional advantageous experience includes antimicrobial time-kill assays, biofilm models, bioluminescent bacterial imaging, pharmacological treatment studies, molecular microbiology, bacterial genetics, and working with clinically relevant bacterial pathogens. The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to data analysis, preparation of reports and manuscripts, programme milestone deliverables, general laboratory activities, and supervision of postgraduate and undergraduate researchers where appropriate. Pūroko | Further details This is a full-time (1.0 FTE), fixed term position for 12 months based in Ōtepoti | Dunedin. The preferred start date is 1 October 2026, although some flexibility in the start date may be possible for the right candidate. The salary for this position is $92,350 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information, please contact Associate Professors Daniel Pletzer or Allan Gamble via the contact details below Tono | Application Applicants should provide •    A short cover letter outlining their suitability for the position. •    A detailed CV highlighting relevant microbiology and in vivo infection experience, publications relevant to the project. •    Names and contact details of at least two academic or scientific referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601348 will close on Wednesday, 16 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Associate Professor Daniel Pletzer Contact: Associate Professor Allan Gamble Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website , Pletzer laboratory and Gamble laboratory Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Postdoctoral Fellow ($92,350) Organisation Microbiology and Immunology Job Function Research ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601232": {
      "title": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Social Anthropology (2601232)",
      "description": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Social Anthropology (2601232) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Social Anthropology (2601232) Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Social Anthropology - 2601232 __________ THE SOCIAL ANTHROPOLOGY PROGRAMME | TE MĀTAI TIKAKA TAKATA DIVISION OF HUMANITIES | TE KETE ARONUI About us | Mō mātou The University of Otago is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities, ranked in the top 1% in the QS World University Rankings. The Social Anthropology Programme operates as a distinctive programme within the School of Social Sciences alongside six others (Archaeology; Media, Film and Communication; Politics; Religion; Social and Community Work; Sociology, Gender Studies and Criminology). The School hosts 80 academic and professional staff engaged in interdisciplinary research and teaching a diverse student body from across the University. Social Anthropology has a strong record of meaningful and engaged teaching and research. The Programme offers a range of research-informed undergraduate and postgraduate papers in medical anthropology, political economy, the anthropology of the Pacific, ethnographic methods, and more. We have an active commitment to Indigenous-focused learning and decolonial initiatives in our curriculum and wider practice. One of our main areas of strength is a focus on medical anthropology, and more broadly on humanistic perspectives on health, illness, and disability. Our programme also recently led the founding of a new research centre, Ngā Aho Hauora | Centre for Medical Humanities (the first such centre in Australasia) which promotes interdisciplinary work, community-focused and applied projects, and opportunities for national and international research collaborations. The role | Te mahi Applications are invited for a Lecturer or Senior Lecturer in Social Anthropology. The position offers an opportunity to join a strong and forward-looking team that is dedicated to excellence. We are looking for a candidate with experience and proven ability in delivering engaging and innovative teaching and research. Key responsibilities of the role include: •    Playing a significant role in the teaching of Health Science 100-level; including a high standard of lecturing, marking, moderation, and student engagement. •    Contributing to the development of Medical Anthropology and/or Social Science perspectives on health, illness, disability, & society, as a specialist area of teaching and research. •    Contributing expertise in the teaching of issues concerning Māori and/or Pasifika health in the Aotearoa/NZ context. •    Contributing to the Social Anthropology curriculum, via teaching into our undergraduate and postgraduate ANTH papers. •    Contributing to broader uptake of te ao Māori and te Tiriti facing perspectives in the Social Anthropology Programme, wider Humanities Division and University, through participation and/or partnership. •    Supporting the new Centre for Medical Humanities | Ngā Aho Hauora We particularly welcome applications from Māori scholars who can support the ongoing development of an integrated curriculum informed by Indigenous pedagogical models, and contribute to expanding opportunities for student assessment in te reo Māori. The successful candidate will also be expected to supervise research at Masters and PhD levels; to develop and maintain a strong research profile, including showing a willingness to seek external research funds; and to undertake administrative roles and community/professional service activities that contribute to the overall effectiveness and standing of the Programme. Skills and Experience | Kā pūkeke me kā wheako The successful candidate is expected to have: •    A PhD in Social Anthropology, or in a related field (with an interest in developing expertise and engagement with the discipline of social anthropology) •    An established record of research and service, with connections to national and international researchers and stakeholder communities. •    A record of delivering high-quality teaching; with a commitment to contributing to team-taught courses, taking on coordination of existing courses, or developing their own specialist courses within our programme as needed. •    Established expertise in social science and/or Indigenous perspectives on health, illness, disability, & society, particularly with a focus on Māori and/or Pasifika health in the Aotearoa/NZ context. •    Expertise and comfort in te ao Māori, and good working use of te reo Māori. •    A demonstrated commitment to collegiality and productive collaboration in the context of a small to medium-sized programme •    Show strong skills in organisation, coordination, and productive communication, including in cross-divisional contexts Early career staff have a managed workload programme to enable them to develop their research activities through the first two years or so of their appointment. Further Information | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time position, based in Ōtepoti Dunedin, and is available from February 2027. The appointment ranges for this role are: •    Lecturer - $97,377 to $ 114,943 per annum. •    Senior Lecturer - $ 120,494 to $137,444 per annum. Appointment level will be determined by the candidate’s academic achievements, skills, and experience. Specific enquiries may be directed to Associate Professor Tim Thomas, Head, School of Social Sciences, via the email address below. Application | Tono Candidates are required to submit the following: •    A CV, including employment history. •    A letter of application outlining how you fit the role. •    Names and addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601232 will close on 11:59pm (NZST) on Wednesday, 30 September 2026. For further information about the Social Anthropology Programme, see: https://www.otago.ac.nz/anthropology Additional Information Contact: Associate Professor Tim Thomas Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow ($97,377 to $114,943) or Senior Lecturer / Senior Research Fellow ($120,494 to $137,444) Organisation Social Anthropology Job Function Other Academic ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601284": {
      "title": "Job Description - Pūkenga | Lecturer - Botany (2601284)",
      "description": "Job Description - Pūkenga | Lecturer - Botany (2601284) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Pūkenga | Lecturer - Botany (2601284) Pūkenga | Lecturer - Botany - 2601284 __________ DEPARTMENT OF BOTANY | TE TARI HUAOTA DIVISION OF SCIENCES | TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA Ko wai mātou | Who we are The Division of Sciences is one of the four teaching divisions in the University, the others being Health Sciences, Commerce, and Humanities. The Division of Sciences represents a broad range of disciplines in 15 Departments and a number of inter-disciplinary programmes. The Department of Botany at the University of Otago based in Ōtepoti Dunedin, Te Wai Pounamu South Island is the only Department of Botany in Aotearoa New Zealand and has a long history, being the first Department of Botany to be formed in this country. Staff in the Department of Botany teach into a wide range of degree majors, including Botany, Ecology, Genetics, Plant Biotechnology, Food Science, Pharmacy, Agricultural Innovation, Marine Science and Oceanography. Our Department has a strong research culture, that fosters collaboration internally, across the university, and with external and international research institutions. Researchers in the department produce high impact theoretical and applied research spanning ecosystem science, plant functional biology, cell and molecular biology and taxonomy, with a strong emphasis on the ecology and evolution of New Zealand flora*, ecosystem processes and services, environmental change and resilience, physiological processes and plant-human interactions, including food (*‘flora’ defined broadly to include terrestrial and aquatic vascular and non-vascular plants, fungi, algae, cyanobacteria and plant viruses). Te mahi | The Role Applications are invited for the position of Lecturer in Botany. We are seeking to appoint a dynamic researcher and teacher with a strong track record of research in plant genetics, plant physiology, plant pathology or a similar field. This might include research in plant stress physiology, developmental genetics, biotechnology, plant virology or other similar fields of research. The applicant should demonstrate how their research aligns with national funding priorities . The successful candidate will pursue and engage in new opportunities that contribute to teaching and research in the Department of Botany. The position affords the opportunity for interested scholars to teach and conduct research in a manner that is transformative and beneficial to Māori or Pasifika. Applicants are encouraged to identify the manner in which they will complement existing strengths in the Department and develop new and innovative directions in research and teaching. Through this appointment we intend to further enable the Department of Botany to strengthen its engagement and commitment to Te Tiriti o Waitangi and the University of Otago’s Māori Strategic Framework and Pacific Strategic Framework. The successful candidate will be an important part of our future plan to grow Māori and Pacifica academics within the Department. Kā pūkeka me kā wheako | Skills and Experience •    A PhD with a research focus in plant genetics, plant physiology, plant pathology, or similar field. •    A strong track record (relative to opportunity) of publishing research in high quality scientific journals, and presenting research at national/international symposia. •    Demonstrated ability to work well with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds. •    Demonstrated ability and passion for delivering high-quality teaching that will support and enhance the Department’s degree programme. •    Ability to establish, develop and maintain an independent and on-going research programme with funding success. •    Have effective communication and relationship management skills, with the ability to create a collegial, supportive, and motivating environment for staff and students. •    Be committed to and/or have established connections or track record of collaboration with international and/or national researchers. •    Ability and desire to participate in service within the department, the University and wider community. Pūroko | Further Details This is a permanent, full-time (1 FTE) position, based in Ōtepoti Dunedin, and is available from February 2027. The appointment range for this role is $97,377 to $ 114,943 per annum. Appointment level will be determined by the candidate’s academic achievements, skills, and experience. Specific enquiries may be directed to Professor David Orlovich, Head of Department, Department of Botany|Te Tari Huaota via the contact details below. Tono | Application Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    A CV. •    Cover letter. •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601284 will close on 11:59 pm (NZST) on Sunday, 27 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Professor David Orlovich Tel: 03 479 9060 Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow ($97,377 to $114,943) Organisation Botany Job Function Research and Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2600772": {
      "title": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor - Prosthodontics (2600772)",
      "description": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor - Prosthodontics (2600772) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor - Prosthodontics (2600772) Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor - Prosthodontics - 2600772 __________ DEPARTMENT OF ORAL REHABILITATION FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities. Founded in 1907, the Faculty of Dentistry is based at the University’s main campus in Dunedin and is the only dental school in New Zealand. It is proud of its international reputation for research, clinical, and academic excellence. The 2026 QS World University Subject Rankings list the Dental School as 50th in the world. We are committed to working closely with iwi and Māori organisations, and mātauraka Māori has become an integral part of teaching and research across the institution. We especially value our relationship with Ngai Tahu, the mana whenua within their takiwā of Te Waipounamu, and the University’s principal Treaty partner. The role | Te mahi The Faculty of Dentistry seeks to appoint a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer/Associate Professor in Prosthodontics. This position provides an excellent opportunity for an academic to establish themselves in one of the leading dental schools in the world. Key tasks for this position include: •    Delivering clinical teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. •    Contributing to curriculum design for undergraduate and postgraduate levels. •    Supervising undergraduate and postgraduate research. •    Undertaking research resulting in quality-assured publications within the discipline, either individually or as part of a group. •    Contributing to the running of the Department, School, Division and the University. You will be supported through a collegial academic environment, an active and dynamic research culture, and through efficient professional services across the University. The Faculty is housed in modern, state-of the art facilities. Academic, research and clinical activities are centred around the Walsh Building and Clinical Services Building in Dunedin, with an additional satellite clinic in Auckland. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful applicant will hold a dental degree and a specialist qualification in Prosthodontics, preferably also a higher research degree (PhD or equivalent), and will demonstrate: •    A track record or potential of internationally peer-reviewed research. •    Teaching skills at undergraduate and postgraduate levels. •    Ability to provide specialist patient care. •    A collaborative and collegial approach to work. •    Full understanding of the principles of the Treaty of Waitangi and the University’s Māori Strategic Framework. You will be a dentist who is registerable with the Dental Council of New Zealand in the specialist scope of Prosthodontics. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (1.0 FTE) position based in Ōtepoti/Dunedin. The salary range for this position is (per annum): •    Lecturer $98,583 to $120,528 •    Senior Lecturer $148,416 to $184,993 •    Associate Professor $195,968 to $212,427 The University of Otago is known for collegiality and combined with living in the vibrant city of Dunedin, provides opportunities for an excellent work-life balance. The Dunedin campus has been ranked as one of the 15 most beautiful in the world. The city has affordable living, excellent schools, and a great array of activities, ranging from art and literature to outdoor activities. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children Act 2014. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. For further information, please contact Farai Madambi, Senior Recruitment Consultant via the email address below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    CV •    Cover letter •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2600772 will close on Sunday, 11 October 2026. Additional Information Contact: Farai Madambi Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Please see information above Organisation Oral Rehabilitation Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601245": {
      "title": "Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow/Research Fellow: Sea Ice Observations (2601245)",
      "description": "Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow/Research Fellow: Sea Ice Observations (2601245) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow/Research Fellow: Sea Ice Observations (2601245) Postdoctoral Fellow/Research Fellow: Sea Ice Observations - 2601245 __________ DEPARTMENT OF PHYSICS - TE TARI HŪ-0-TE-KŌHAO About our team | Mō te tīma The University of Otago, established in 1869, has a long and distinguished tradition of teaching and research. The Physics Department of the University of Otago was the first Physics Department established in New Zealand. Today, the Department embraces innovative teaching methods, promotes a vigorous research ethos, and is committed both to Physics as a discipline and to its applications. Our key objectives are to provide students with the highest quality education, and to produce research of international excellence. The Department of Physics at the University of Otago has a vibrant research environment, with strength in sea ice physics and geoscience research as well pure and applied physics, electronics and energy studies. The role | Te mahi This Postdoctoral Fellow/Research Fellow position is an exciting opportunity for an early career sea ice researcher to join one of the world’s leading sea ice observational research groups to collaborate on the systematically compiling sea ice, pack ice observations from the Scientific Committee on Antarctic Research’s sea ice expert group Antarctic Sea Ice Processes and Climate (ASPeCt) and fast ice records through the Antarctic Fast ice Network (AFIN). The position is part of SYNCHRONY (Synchronous Southern Ocean Observatory), a Horizon Europe project advancing a coordinated, multidisciplinary and sustainable observing system for the Southern Ocean and Antarctica. SYNCHRONY contributes to Antarctica InSync, a broader international initiative coordinating circumpolar and year-round observations to strengthen our understanding of the Antarctic environment. As a Postdoctoral Fellow/Research Fellow, you will be joining a multidisciplinary, international research team of physicists, climate modellers, oceanographers, and atmospheric scientists contributing to Antarctica InSync. You will play a central role in delivering the compilations of sea ice observational data. This will involve working closely with Dr Smith and international collaborators from the SYNCHRONY project. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful applicant must have: •    A PhD (or equivalent) already awarded, with the thesis and/or international peer-reviewed publications in sea ice physics or a related physical sciences discipline such as oceanography, remote sensing, or meteorology. •    Excellent data management and computing skills. •    The ability to engage with and work with a wide variety of international researchers and stakeholders in the compilation of disparate sea ice observational data. •    The ability to work without direct supervision, set priorities and manage their own time effectively in order to achieve the goals of the project whilst maintaining a healthy work-life balance. •    A willingness to engage in two-way discussions related to Vision Mātauranga. Taiao (environmental sustainability) is a core part of Vision Mātauranga: https://www.mbie.govt.nz/assets/9916d28d7b/vision-matauranga-booklet.pdf. •    A willingness and the ability to support PhD, MSc, honours, and/or summer research students who may be involved with the project. Preferred qualities for the successful applicant are: •    Sea ice related fieldwork experience in Antarctica and/or the Southern Ocean. •    Familiarity with ASPeCt and/or AFIN protocols and data sets Further details | Pūroko This position is part-time (0.8 FTE), fixed-term appointment for one year based in Dunedin. The hours of work are negotiable with the successful candidate; however, candidates needing a work visa will be required to work 0.8 FTE. Candidates with the right to live and work in New Zealand may be able to negotiate reduced hours of work, which would proportionately extend the duration of the fixed-term appointment. The salary for Postdoctoral Fellow is $92,350 per annum (pro-rata). The salary range for Research Fellow is $97,377 - $114,943 per annum (pro-rata). Appointment level will be determined by the candidate’s academic achievements, skills, and experience. Specific enquiries may be directed to Professor Inga Smith, Department of Physics via the contact details below: Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit: •    A copy of your full CV including a list of publications. •    A one-page cover letter detailing how your skills and experience match those required for the position and why you are interested in the position. •    Names and contact details of three referees. Additional material may be requested for short-listed applicants but should not be submitted at the initial application stage. To submit your application, please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601245 will close at 11:59PM NZST on Sunday, 20th September 2026. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Professor Inga Smith Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow ($97,377 to $114,943) Organisation Physics Job Function Research ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601094": {
      "title": "Job Description - Business Support Coordinator - Dodd Walls Centre (2601094)",
      "description": "Job Description - Business Support Coordinator - Dodd Walls Centre (2601094) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Business Support Coordinator - Dodd Walls Centre (2601094) Business Support Coordinator - Dodd Walls Centre - 2601094 __________ TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA | DIVISION OF SCIENCES TE WHAI AO | DODD-WALLS CENTRE FOR PHOTONIC AND QUANTUM TECHNOLOGIES •    Salary + 6.75% Superannuation + 5 weeks annual leave. •    Career Development + tertiary study benefit + ongoing professional development opportunities. •    Be part of a professional and supportive team that facilitates world-class research. About our team | Mō te tīma Te Whai Ao — Dodd-Walls Centre, is a New Zealand Centre of Research Excellence (CoRE) specializing in the science of light, spanning fundamental quantum physics to cutting-edge optical technologies. Bringing together students and researchers from universities across Aotearoa, the Centre focuses on quantum optics, photonics, biophotonics and precision atomic physics. The Centre engages in educational outreach and commercialisation activities. The role | Te mahi Due to an internal move, we are seeking a new Business Support Co-ordinator to play a pivotal role in supporting the operational success of Te Whai Ao – Dodd Walls Centre.  Working closely with the Programme Manager, you will provide high-quality business and administrative support. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced administrator or coordinator who enjoys a varied workload, building relationships and contributing to the success of a nationally significant research centre. Key responsibilities include: •    Providing administrative and operational support to the Programme Manager. •    Compiling statistics, conducting data analysis, and drafting policies and reports to support operational and annual reporting requirements. •    Servicing committees, and overseeing financial transactions, reconciliations, and funding awards. •    Supporting communications activities, including newsletters and website updates. •    Planning and coordinating logistics, including travel arrangements for major events, notably the annual symposium. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You will have a relevant tertiary qualification or equivalent administrative experience, demonstrating exceptional attention to detail and a strong work ethic. Reliability and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a small team are essential.   You will be highly organised, able to manage competing priorities in a fast-paced environment. Other key skills include: •    Exceptional communication skills and ability to build relationships with people at all levels. •    Ability to interpret data and assist with reporting and policy writing. •    A calm, adaptable, and flexible approach, with a commitment to continuous improvement. •    Expertise in Microsoft Office Suite, with ability to adapt and navigate new systems. •    Experience in event organisation. •    Commitment to working in a culturally safe and respectful manner, with an understanding and appreciation of Te Ao Māori, Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles, and Pasifika values. Prior experience working within a tertiary, research or a research-focused environment would be an advantage. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours), permanent position, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointments range for this role is $69,931 to $74,609. Top of the range is $80,285, per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that we honour Te Tiriti o Waitangi and that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence – please contact Yashoda Lodhia, Programme Manager, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter), please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601094 will close on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Yashoda Lodhia Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 5 (Range of Appointments $69,931 to $74,609, Top of Range $80,285) Organisation Sciences Divisional Office Job Function Administrative ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601292": {
      "title": "Job Description - Specialist Cyber Security Assurance (2601292)",
      "description": "Job Description - Specialist Cyber Security Assurance (2601292) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Specialist Cyber Security Assurance (2601292) Specialist Cyber Security Assurance - 2601292 __________ IT ASSURANCE AND CYBER SECURITY | WHAKAŪ HANGARAU, WHAKAMARU IPURANGI DIGITAL SERVICES DIVISION | RATONGA MATIHIKO Come join the University and be part of its success as a key member of the University's Cyber Security and Identity team. About us | Mō mātou Here at Digital Services we are responsible for the provision, maintenance, and support of information and communication technologies and business systems within the University of Otago community. We are one of the largest ICT operations in NZ, supporting 27,000 users, a big network across multiple campuses and a huge range of systems. The role | Te mahi As Specialist Cyber Security Assurance you'll act as a trusted internal advisor on security controls, consulting on and assessing cyber security assurance activities across Otago's digital projects and information assets. You'll assist teams in following a secure-by-design approach, applying and validating security controls to ensure our information assets are protected. As part of the wider Cyber Security and Identity team, you will have a strong support network and a wide range of opportunities in other areas including cyber operations as well as identity and access management. This role is also about partnership and influence. You'll advise digital projects on appropriate security controls and collaborate with stakeholders across the University to embed a culture of security and compliance. Key responsibilities include: •    Consulting on, assessing, reporting on, and tracking cyber security assurance activities across digital projects. •    Testing and validating the application of security controls using a secure-by-design approach. •    Managing stakeholder relationships and engaging with projects to help them deploy secure solutions. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Ideally, you will be an experienced and proactive cyber security professional with a strong technical background and the confidence to engage effectively across a wide range of stakeholders. You’ll bring solid industry experience, a clear understanding of security frameworks, and the ability to provide practical, consultative guidance that supports organisational security outcomes. The successful candidate will also: •    Hold a tertiary qualification in Computer Science or a related field, with proven experience in information or cyber security. •    Demonstrate experience applying recognised security frameworks such as NIST CSF, CIS Critical Controls, or HISF. •    Bring proven stakeholder engagement capability with a collaborative, partnership focused approach. •    Have hands on experience in technical cyber security consulting and implementing security controls. •    Have a strong understanding of Disaster Recovery and Business Continuity. •    Ideally hold postgraduate study or certifications such as CIA, CISA, SC 100, CISSP, or CompTIA Security+. •    Show a track record of driving continuous improvement in large or complex environments. •    Understand New Zealand regulatory requirements and public sector governance. •    Bring familiarity with Te Reo Māori, tikanga and mātauranga Māori in a workplace context. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours), permanent position. We have a strong preference that the role is located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin, however we will consider work from one of our satellite campuses. We offer a competitive salary, five weeks' annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Candidates who successfully reach the final stages of the selection process will be required to undergo a police vetting check. A satisfactory report from the relevant agency will be a condition of employment. For further information regarding the role please contact Andrew Reed, via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601292 will close on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Andrew Reed Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Cyber Security and Identity Job Function Systems Architecture and Administration ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601304": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies",
      "description": "Job Description - Warden/Tautiaki - University Flats (2601304) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Warden/Tautiaki - University Flats (2601304) Warden/Tautiaki - University Flats - 2601304 __________ UNIVERSITY FLATS | WHARE MAHA KĀIKA TAHI CAMPUS AND COLLEGIATE LIFE SERVICES | TE URU KAHIKA The University of Otago is New Zealand's first and oldest University. It has a strong teaching and research tradition and well-established national and international linkages. UniFlats is a residential flatting community which provides around 700 rooms in more than 180 separate properties. The UniFlats mission is to provide and strengthen high-quality accommodation for international, postgraduate, and other students at the University of Otago, creating a safe, inclusive, and academically supportive living environment. Grounded in the principles of Manaakitanga (care, respect, and generosity of spirit), UniFlats fosters a collegial community that promotes wellbeing and belonging while working in partnership across the University to contribute meaningfully to positive student outcomes. The role | Te mahi The Warden of University Flats provides leadership and operational oversight of a large, residential accommodation portfolio with full accountability for service delivery, financial performance, regulatory compliance, risk management, and the leadership of a multidisciplinary staffing team. Key responsibilities include portfolio-wide budget management, resource allocation, systems and process optimisation, resident selection and allocations, maintaining property and safety standards, and ensuring a high-quality residential experience aligned with University strategy. The Warden drives operational excellence while cultivating a culture of accountability, professionalism, and continuous improvement across the Uniflats community. UniFlats is a busy year-round operation of strategic importance, particularly in providing accommodation for international students. As a result, the Warden will collaborate closely with the International Office and Student Services. We seek an experienced leader who is adept in building and leading a culture of high-quality service delivery, sustainable innovation, consistency and continuous improvement. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Our ideal candidate will: •    Have a track record in outstanding leadership, management, budget and organisation skills. •    Have the ability to perform effectively and professionally in a complex and challenging environment. •    Be familiar with a culture of working with International and Postgraduate students. •    Have strong administrative, organisational and financial skills. •    Have highly developed communication skills, using multiple media. •    Be able to prioritise resources to competing priorities effectively. •    Contribute more widely to the Campus and Collegiate Life Services Division. A relevant degree level tertiary qualification or commensurate experience is essential, with a preference for a candidate who has had experience working with tertiary students. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week) permanent position, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $107,320 to $115,834. Top of range $124,348. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children’s Act 2014. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a police safety check. Confidential enquiries can be directed to Dr Sean McCallaghan, Senior Warden of Colleges, Campus & Collegiate Life Services Division via the contact information below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601304 will close on Sunday, 6 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Dr Sean McCallaghan Tel: 021 2791130 Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Management Band 3 (Range of Appointments $107,320 to $115,834, Top of Range $124,348) Organisation University Flats Job Function Administrative and Operations Management ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601286": {
      "title": "Job Description - Systems Specialist (2601286)",
      "description": "Job Description - Systems Specialist (2601286) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Systems Specialist (2601286) Systems Specialist - 2601286 __________ DIGITAL SERVICES DIVISION | RATONGA MATIHIKO Te mahi | The role If you are passionate about digital learning and are seeking an opportunity to make a difference, why not bring your skills to our Digital Learning team in this Systems Specialist role. Our ideal candidate will have a passion for digital technology in a learning context and will be able to understand systems and pickup systems administration skills quickly for new technology. Our systems specialists work in small nimble teams working across a range of systems in each of our product portfolios. We currently have a vacancy in our Digital Learning Environment Value Stream. As a Systems Specialist, you will: •    Provide technical support for systems, including troubleshooting issues, resolving problems, and working closely with our clients. •    Be responsible for maintaining and administering systems within your portfolio to ensure optimal performance and successful client outcomes. •    Collaborate with our clients to help them achieve their objectives and identify opportunities for continuous improvement. Kā pūkeka me kā wheako | Skills and Experience You will have a good information systems background, a proactive mindset, the ability to work independently, and the capacity to adapt to changing technology and business requirements. We’re looking for someone with experience in systems administration and management who can: •    Deliver great outcomes across our core learning management system and teaching tools. •    Translate requirements into documented, trackable changes. •    Solve complex problems with effective solutions. •    Communicate clearly with stakeholders at all levels. •    Adapt to a fast-paced environment and manage multiple priorities. Mō te tīma | About our team At the University of Otago, you would be joining the team responsible for empowering the future of New Zealand thinkers every day. You will be making learning easier for 21,000 students, setting 5,000+ staff up for success, and enabling the ground-breaking research which kick-starts innovations to benefit generations to come. Pūroko | Further details This is a permanent, full-time (37.5 hours per week) position, located on the Ōtepoti/Dunedin Campus. The appointment range is $85,057 to $92,174 per annum. Top of the range is $97,508. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. You must have a right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence – please contact Leah McKay via the email address below. Tono | Application To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601285 will close on Wednesday, 09 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Leah McKay Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Digital Specialist Band C (Range of Appointments $85,057 to $92,174, Top of Range $97,508) Organisation Information Systems Job Function Business and Systems Analyst ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601310": {
      "title": "Job Description - Kitchen Assistant (2601310)",
      "description": "Job Description - Kitchen Assistant (2601310) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Kitchen Assistant (2601310) Kitchen Assistant - 2601310 __________ CAMPUS AND COLLEGIATE LIFE SERVICES | TE URU KAHIKA UNIVERSITY UNION | TE UNIANA O ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA About our team/Mō te tīma College Catering is one of New Zealand's largest institutional food service operations, producing more than 8,500 meals each day across eleven University Colleges. Our team takes pride in providing quality food and service that supports the student experience and contributes to Otago's vibrant collegiate community. The role/Te mahi We are seeking a reliable and motivated Kitchen Assistant to join our busy Central Production Kitchen team. Working in a fast-paced and supportive environment, you'll play an important role in helping deliver high-quality meals to thousands of students each day. Key responsibilities •    Washing dishes, trays, utensils, and kitchen equipment. •    Maintaining a clean, safe, and organised kitchen environment. •    Assisting with basic food preparation as required. •    Supporting chefs and kitchen staff with daily tasks. •    Following food safety and health and safety procedures. •    Receiving and storing deliveries. •    Assisting with end-of-day cleaning and shutdown procedures. What we offer •    Stable, permanent employment within a well-established organisation. •    A supportive and inclusive team environment. •    Opportunities to develop food service and hospitality skills. •    Semester-time working arrangements. Further details/Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time, semester-time-only position based in Dunedin, working during the academic year (February to November). Hours of work are 40 hours per week, worked on a rostered basis. The successful candidate will commence in September 2026. The appointment range is $26.43 to $27.48 per hour. While this position will initially be based in the Central Production Kitchen, it may be relocated to another University College on the Dunedin campus to meet operational requirements. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information, or to discuss the role in confidence, please contact Executive Chef Charlie Mancey via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including a CV and cover letter), please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference 2601310 will close on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Applications will be reviewed as they are received, and candidates may be invited to interview before the closing date. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. #LI-DNI Additional Information Contact: Charlie Mancey Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Assistant - Outlet/Kitchen/Cafe/Retail/Stores (Range of Appointments $26.43 to $27.48 per hour, Top of Range $27.48) Organisation Catering Job Function Food and Hospitality Services ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601260": {
      "title": "Job Description - Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer)  (2601260)",
      "description": "Job Description - Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer)  (2601260) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer)  (2601260) Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer) - 2601260 __________ DEPARTMENT OF SURGERY AND CRITICAL CARE | TE TARI HĀPARAPARA, TE TARI WHĀOMOOMO DUNEDIN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE | TE KURA WHAIORA A ŌTEPOTI Are you an enthusiastic motivated Sonographer wishing to work in the area of vascular disease? Then this job is for you. We invite you to apply for this full-time position of Clinical Vascular Scientist (Sonographer) in the Otago Vascular Diagnostics Assessment Unit within the Department of Surgery and Critical Care. About our team/Mō te tīma We are a team of vascular surgeons, technologists, vascular sonographers and research staff using various diagnostic investigations to research the causes and management of vascular conditions. Our unit provides clinical diagnostic services to the Southern District Health Board, and to the wider community, to assist in the diagnosis and treatment of vascular diseases. The unit is also part of the Vascular Research Group and works closely with other research groups at local, national and international levels. The role/Te mahi In this role you will join a multidisciplinary focused clinical research team investigating vascular disease. This position involves working within a fun team of vascular technicians and sonographers in assessing patients with vascular disease, using physiological techniques and ultrasound. Assessments are carried out for the purpose of both clinical patient care and research. The successful applicant would learn the specific techniques and assist in the general tasks of the Assessment Unit. Skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate would be expected and supported to support clinical research and teaching, along with clinical ultrasound services to Health New Zealand | Te Whatu Ora. You will have: •    A post graduate diploma in medical ultrasound (DMU) or an equivalent recognised qualification (We may consider a new graduate with specific vascular training). •    To be registrable with the Medical Radiation Technologists Board (MRTB). •    Good organisational skills. •    A professional approach and ability to relate well when dealing with the public. •    High standards of both oral and written communication skills. Further details/Pūroko This is a permanent, full time (37.5 hours per week) position, based in Dunedin, commencing in October 2026. Part-time hours may also be considered. The appointment range for this role is within an agreed salary range, applicable to this department and will be determined based on the successful candidates qualifications, skills and relevant experience. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information please contact Brigid G. Hill via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference 2601260 will close on Sunday, 27 September 2026 Applications may be reviewed as they are received, the University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Ms Brigid (Gerry) Hill Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Contact Department for Information Organisation Surgery and Critical Care Job Function Medical and Science Technicians ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2600844": {
      "title": "Job Description - Vascular Laboratory Technician / Trainee Vascular Sonographer (2600844)",
      "description": "Job Description - Vascular Laboratory Technician / Trainee Vascular Sonographer (2600844) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Vascular Laboratory Technician / Trainee Vascular Sonographer (2600844) Vascular Laboratory Technician / Trainee Vascular Sonographer - 2600844 __________ DEPARTMENT OF SURGERY AND CRITICAL CARE | TE TARI HĀPARAPARA, TE TARI WHĀOMOOMO DUNEDIN SCHOOL OF MEDICINE | TE KURA WHAIORA A ŌTEPOTI Are you an enthusiastic motivated person wishing to work in the area of vascular disease? Then this job is for you. We invite you to apply for this full-time position of Vascular Laboratory Technician in the Otago Vascular Diagnostics Unit within the Section of Surgery. About our team/Mō te tīma We are a team of vascular surgeons, technologists and research staff using various diagnostic investigations to research the causes and management of vascular conditions. Our unit provides clinical diagnostic services to the Te Whatu Ora - Southern, and to the wider community, to assist in the diagnosis and treatment of vascular diseases. The unit is also part of the Vascular Research Group and works closely with other research groups at local, national and international levels. The role/Te mahi In this role you will join a multidisciplinary focused clinical research team investigating vascular disease. This position involves working within a fun team of vascular technicians and sonographers in assessing patients with vascular disease, using physiological techniques and ultrasound. Assessments are carried out for the purpose of both clinical patient care and research. The successful applicant would be supported through a two-year post-graduate training programme to learn the specific techniques alongside assisting in the general tasks of the Assessment Unit. Skills and experience/Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Depending on the qualification of the successful candidate, the intention of the role is to study towards a Vascular Ultrasound qualification and/or a research qualification. You will also have: •    Good organisational skills. •    A professional approach and ability to relate well when dealing with the public. •    High standards of both oral and written communication skills. Further details/Pūroko This is a fixed term, full time (37.5 hours per week) position, based in Dunedin, commencing in October 2026 to facilitate introduction to the role and the training pathway. The duration of the training programme is 2 years and commences in February/ March 2027. The position is structured to include 2 years post-qualification if applicable. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. The appointment range for this role is $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640 per annum. If part-time, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information please contact Brigid G. Hill via the contact details below. Application/Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference 2600844 will close on Sunday, 27 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received, the University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Ms Brigid (Gerry) Hill Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Level 4 (Range of Appointments $63,508 to $68,185, Top of Range $73,640) Organisation Surgery and Critical Care Job Function Medical and Science Technicians ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601239": {
      "title": "Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Psychology (2601239)",
      "description": "Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Psychology (2601239) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Psychology (2601239) Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Psychology - 2601239 __________ DEPARTMENT OF PSYCHOLOGY | TE TARI WHAKAMĀTAU HINEKARO DIVISION OF SCIENCES | TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA About our team | Mō te tīma The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka has been a renowned place of learning and discovery for more than 150 years. We strive to become an even more successful, globally influential university, committed to upholding the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi as we advance outstanding research and research-informed teaching to enable transformative learning and student experiences. Our Vision / Ko te Aronga is for our people and knowledge to transform lives, connect communities and nurture the environment in Aotearoa, the Pacific, and the world. The Department of Psychology has an excellent research culture and is one of the top Psychology programmes in the world, since the inception of the QS world rankings. We boast excellent computing and technical facilities which support the department’s large and varied teaching and research programmes. Our department provides two professional psychology postgraduate programmes.  Our Postgraduate Diploma in Clinical Psychology (PgDipClPs) is a pathway for students to be eligible to register with the New Zealand Psychologists’ Board in the Clinical Psychology scope of practice.  Our Postgraduate Diploma in Psychological Assessment and Intervention (PgDipPAI) is a pathway for students to be eligible to register in the Psychology Assistant scope of practice. The role | Te mahi We invite applications from suitably qualified and enthusiastic candidates for Professional Practice Fellow or Senior Professional Practice Fellow positions within the Department of Psychology. The successful candidates will contribute to teaching, training, supervision, and programme development across the Department’s professional psychology programmes. Key responsibilities include: •    Delivering teaching and assessment across the PgDipClPs and PgDipPAI programmes. •    Providing clinical supervision to psychology students in the Psychology Clinic and other practice settings. •    Contributing to course coordination, programme administration, and selection and examination panels. •    Integrating culturally responsive and research-informed practice into teaching and clinical training. •    Developing clinical learning opportunities and provide clinic cover as required. This is a unique opportunity for an energetic, creative, and committed individual to make a significant impression and contribution in their chosen discipline while being well supported by a collegial and dynamic Division and University. The successful candidate will be appointed as either a Professional Practice Fellow or Senior Professional Practice Fellow, dependent on skills and experience. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako To be successful in this role candidates must be a New Zealand-registered Psychologist, preferably within the Clinical Psychology or Neuropsychology scope of practice. Candidates will have at least five years’ relevant clinical experience, preferably with most of this experience in mental health settings. Candidates are also expected to have: •    Appropriate professional experience. •    Excellence in case conceptualisation, formulation, and diagnosis. •    A commitment to the principles of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and to equal opportunity. •    Established relationships with the local clinical community is desirable. •    Evidence of an ability to work well with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds and at all levels of the institution. Preference may be given to candidates who can provide appropriate cultural supervision and support to staff and students, as well as those with demonstrated ability in teaching and training in areas of current programme needs. We are particularly keen to hear from candidates who can support the programme in developing students’ competence to work in a culturally safe manner with Māori and Pasifika clients. Further details | Pūroko We have up to 2.0 FTE positions available, with options for both permanent and fixed term appointments, at full-time or part-time hours. Hours of work will be negotiated with the successful candidates. Please indicate your preferred employment type and FTE in your cover letter. These positions are available from October 2026 based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this role. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children's Act 2014. Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. Specific enquiries about the position may be directed to Associate Professor Deirdre Brown (Clinical Programme Director) and Professor Richard Linscott via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    CV. •    Cover letter. •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601239 will close on Thursday, 17 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Associate Professor Deirdre Brown Tel: +64 3 556 6416 Contact: Professor Richard Linscott Tel: +64 3 479 5689 Position details: Professional Practice Fellow & Senior Professional Practice Fellow Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) or 2 ($91,614 to $102,900) or 3 ($105,665 to $114,943) or 4 ($120,495 to $137,444) Organisation Psychology Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601285": {
      "title": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Marketing (2601285)",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Marketing (2601285) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Marketing (2601285) Teaching Fellow - Marketing - 2601285 __________ DEPARTMENT OF MARKETING | TE MĀTAURANGA TOKOKA UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO | ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago | Ōtākou Whakaihu Waka is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities, ranked among the top 1% globally in the QS World University Rankings. The Otago Business School is AACSB- and EQUIS-accredited and recognised as one of Australasia’s leading business schools, offering a range of undergraduate, postgraduate, PhD, and DBA programmes that combine research excellence with a commitment to developing graduates who can think critically and make meaningful contributions. The Department of Marketing within the Otago Business School at the University of Otago is a dynamic and collegial academic community recognised nationally and internationally for excellence in teaching, research, and engagement. The Department offers a comprehensive range of undergraduate and postgraduate programmes that emphasise critical thinking, creativity, and real-world application. Staff are research-active scholars whose work contributes to advancing marketing theory and practice, with strengths in sustainability, consumer behaviour, services marketing, and social marketing. The Department fosters a supportive and inclusive culture that values innovation in teaching, interdisciplinary collaboration, and engagement with industry and community partners. The role | Te mahi The Department of Marketing is seeking to appoint a permanent, 0.8 FTE Teaching Fellow to contribute to the delivery and development of undergraduate marketing teaching. This is an academic year only position, with teaching responsibilities from February to October each year. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching across a range of marketing courses, including MART 112, MART 201, MART 207, MART 211, MART 212, and MART 308. The role will contribute to high-quality teaching, course coordination and/or delivery, assessment, student engagement, and the ongoing enhancement of the Department’s Marketing curriculum. The successful candidate will work collaboratively with colleagues to provide engaging and effective learning experiences and support students throughout their studies. This role will suit an enthusiastic and student-focused educator with expertise across core areas of marketing and a commitment to innovative, inclusive, and evidence-based teaching and learning. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate will have: •    A degree in Marketing and demonstrated knowledge across core areas of marketing. •    Demonstrated tertiary teaching experience in Marketing, including undergraduate teaching. •    Demonstrated understanding of tertiary pedagogy and research-informed teaching practice. •    Experience in course delivery and/or coordination, including assessment and curriculum development or enhancement. •    Demonstrated experience in designing and assessing student learning, including providing constructive feedback. •    Demonstrated ability to engage and support students from diverse backgrounds and contribute to an inclusive learning environment. •    Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively both independently and collaboratively. •    A commitment to upholding the values, tikanga, kawa, and traditions of Ngāi Tahu as mana whenua, and to working respectfully within the cultural context of the region. Further details | Pūroko This is a permanent, part-time (0.8 FTE), academic year only position, based in Ōtepoti Dunedin. The academic year is from February to October each year, the exact start and end dates will be confirmed each year. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Dunedin is a vibrant university city with a strong student culture, excellent facilities, and a unique natural environment. The city offers a great lifestyle, with easy access to beaches, outdoor recreation, arts, music, restaurants, and cafés. The University of Otago is committed to Te Tiriti as set out in Pae Tata and Vision 2040. We welcome applications from candidates who bring diverse perspectives and experiences and are committed to providing an inclusive and supportive environment for all staff. Confidential enquiries about the role may be directed to Professor Lisa McNeill, Head of Department, Marketing, via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    A CV •    A cover letter •    3 - 4 Referee contact details, including telephone number and email address To submit your application please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601285 will close on Thursday, 10 September 2026. Additional Information Note: As a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range listed. Contact: Professor Lisa McNeill Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow 1 ($75,727 to $87,849) Organisation Marketing Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601229": {
      "title": "Job Description - Specialist Identity and Access Management (2601229)",
      "description": "Job Description - Specialist Identity and Access Management (2601229) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Specialist Identity and Access Management (2601229) Specialist Identity and Access Management - 2601229 __________ CYBER SECURITY AND IDENTITY DIGITAL SERVICES | RATONGA MATIHIKO Come and join the Identity and Access Management team at the University and be part of our Identity transformation journey. About us | Mō mātou Here at Digital Services, we are responsible for the provision, maintenance, and support of information and communication technologies and business systems within the University of Otago community. We are one of the largest ICT operations in NZ, supporting 27,000 users, a big network across multiple campuses and a huge range of systems. The role | Te mahi As a Specialist - Identity and Access Management (IAM) you’ll be part of the Identity Team and a key player in our journey to modernise our core identity systems. You’ll provide advice to University peers around solution design, integration, testing and automation and ensure we provide seamless and secure user experiences. Being involved in the Identity program you will help develop IAM solutions for user provisioning, authentication and authorisation, all while ensuring the current systems remain functional. You’ll apply your knowledge and experience to ensure user identities are securely managed from onboarding to offboarding and any lifecycle events between, ensuring our University community have access to the right things at the right time. You will assist in developing and enforcing RBAC, security protocols and access audits, proactively identifying gaps and developing solutions to issues. You’ll keep up to date with emerging trends and technology to ensure our systems and solutions remain current, secure, and align with business needs and policies. This role is about technical expertise and collaboration. You’ll be part of a focused team helping to ensure our digital access processes remain secure, modern and support the University to ensure the success of our whole community. Key responsibilities include: •    Advising digital peers and projects on identity and access management best practices and solutions. •    Application of technical skills to design and implement IAM solutions and integrations. •    Working collaboratively across specialist teams to ensure end-to-end identity services are delivered effectively and consistently. •    Balancing security with the delivery of a strong user experience. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Essential: •    Tertiary qualification in a relevant discipline (e.g. Computer Science, Cyber Security) or recognised qualifications appropriate to the role (CISSP, CIAM, Microsoft Identity certifications). •    Considerable experience delivering IAM services in a medium-large or complex organisation. •    Strong experience with IGA lifecycle processes (provisioning/deprovisioning, role modelling, access governance, authentication/authorisation). •    Proven experience integrating IAM systems with enterprise applications, cloud environments and on-premise systems. •    Experience configuring connectors and workflows between source and target systems. Preferred: •    Advanced or specialist qualifications in IAM, cyber security, cloud architecture or related domains. •    Demonstrated troubleshooting and problem management capability. •    Experience in Privileged Access Management platforms. •    Proven ability to interpret requirements and contribute to IAM design. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position. We have a strong preference that the role is located in Ōtepoti/Dunedin, however we will consider remote work. While the appointment range for this role is $99,364 to $107,888 (Top of the range $114,280) per annum, the exact salary is to be determined with the successful candidate. We offer five weeks’ annual leave, a 6.75% superannuation scheme, and the ability to achieve real work/life balance. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. Candidates who successfully reach the final stages of the selection process for this role will be required to undergo criminal convictions screening. A satisfactory report from the relevant agency will be a condition of employment. For further information regarding the role please contact John Steele, via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601229 will close on Sunday, 6 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: John Steele Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Digital Specialist Band D (Range of Appointments $99,364 to $107,888, Top of Range $114,280) Organisation Cyber Security and Identity Job Function Systems Architecture and Administration ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601193": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies",
      "description": "Job Description - Student Adviser (2601193) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Student Adviser (2601193) Student Adviser - 2601193 __________ STUDENT DEVELOPMENT ACADEMIC DIVISION | TE WĀHAKA MATUA MĀTAURAKA Who we are | Mō te tīma We are a team of highly skilled professional staff who are part of the Academic Division and are responsible for delivering an outstanding student and staff experience through high quality, effective service delivery to all of the University’s internal and external customers. Our specific focus is on the student experience felt by our students here at the University of Otago. We work to provide accurate and timely information, assistance and advice across a range of activities from admission to graduation. Our wider Academic Division is responsible for strategic oversight of teaching and learning policy and practice, oversight of academic staff confirmation and promotion, and other teams which provide support to our student community. The role | Te mahi You will be working as part of a team, providing high quality advice for undergraduate students ensuring they go on to have an outstanding University of Otago experience. You will provide programme and course information along with guidance to a diverse group of undergraduate students as well as assisting students to make informed choices that support the achievement of their academic and career goals. Range of tasks include: •    Providing holistic, student-centric advice to help students meet their goals and aspirations while at the University of Otago. •    Providing accurate course advice and guidance that complies with all regulations. •    Helping students with information regarding the opportunities and support available such as: Career Development Centre, Disability Information and Support, International Student Support Pacfic Island Centre; Te Huka Mātauraka etc. •    Working closely with teams across Student Experience, Academic Division, Academic staff, and the wider university to provide early stage targeted advice and assistance to a variety of students. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako •    Previous advisory experience. •    A passion for customer service and delivering exceptional service to students. •    Experience providing student administration or related services in a tertiary education organisation. •    Exposure to work in a busy or complex environment. •    A clear understanding of pastoral care and extracurricular services offered at Otago. •    Strong Microsoft office and PC skills (eVision a plus). Due to the nature of the Academic Division, the range of tasks and responsibilities is broad and may require performing duties at different workplaces or locations across campus. We are committed to on-going training and development in order to upskill successful candidates in areas that they desire more exposure to. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time (37.5 hours per week), permanent position, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range is $69,931 to $74,609, top of the range is $80,285 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that we honour Te Tiriti o Waitangi and that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information, please contact Andrew Campbell, Team Leader Student Development via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601193 will close on Sunday, 6 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Andrew Campbell Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 5 (Range of Appointments $69,931 to $74,609, Top of Range $80,285) Organisation Student Experience Job Function Administrative ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601012": {
      "title": "Job Description - Teaching Academic - Surgery and Critical Care (Wellington) (2601012)",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Academic - Surgery and Critical Care (Wellington) (2601012) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Academic - Surgery and Critical Care (Wellington) (2601012) Teaching Academic - Surgery and Critical Care (Wellington) - 2601012 __________ DEPARTMENT OF SURGERY AND CRITICAL CARE / TE TARI HĀPARAPARA, TE TARI WHĀOMOOMO UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO, WELLINGTON / TE WHARE WĀNANGA O ŌTAKOU KI PŌNEKE About our team | Mō te tīma The University of Otago, Wellington (UOW) provides health research and teaching in New Zealand’s capital city. It enjoys collaborative relationships with District Health Boards, local health service providers, multiple central and local government agencies such as the Ministry of Health and Wellington City Council as well as other tertiary education providers such as Massey and Victoria University. It also has strong links with local iwi. Te Tari Hāparapara, Te Tari Whāomoomo / the Department of Surgery and Critical Care at the School of Medicine and Health Sciences, University of Otago, Wellington is a multidisciplinary group dedicated to linking biomedical science with clinical research and providing high-quality clinical teaching. The department has research interests spanning cancer, cardiovascular disease, emergency medicine, clinical service provision, and medical education. Teaching in the department includes Surgery and Critical Care modules in the MB ChB programme, and vocationally focused postgraduate qualifications. The role | Te mahi These Lecturer/Senior Lecturer OR Professional Practice Fellow/Senior Professional Practice Fellow roles within our Department represents an exciting opportunity to make a significant contribution to on-going teaching of postgraduate clinical physiology students and medical students. In this role you will be expected to convene and teach within the Postgraduate Certificate and Diploma courses in Medical Technology. The courses are distance taught and aim to provide the academic framework required for those working as a cardiac or sleep physiologist. These courses provide grounding in underlying physiology and pathophysiology for cardiovascular and sleep medicine. In addition, a focus of the programme is on physiological measurement of cardiovascular, respiratory and sleep parameters. The key responsibility for this role will include coordinating and teaching MTEC701 and 702 which are the papers required for the Postgraduate Certificate in Medical Technology. There will be opportunity to contribute to teaching of medical students within the Department of Surgery and Critical Care. The successful candidate for the Lecturer/Senior Lecturer role will be expected to develop an independent research programme, supervise postgraduate research students, and contribute to departmental and school service activities. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako The successful candidate will have: •    A qualification in medicine or physiology and relevant clinical experience OR a research qualification (PhD or equivalent) in a field relevant to clinical physiology. •    Expertise in either cardiovascular or sleep medicine fields, or general physiology. •    Experience in teaching within a healthcare or academic environment. •    Commitment to collegiality, teamwork, collaboration and cooperation (both internally and externally) in teaching, supervision and research. •    Excellent oral and written communication skills. You will be expected to commit to developing an understanding of te Tiriti o Waitangi, particularly in relation to health outcomes and the University Māori Strategic Framework. Candidates for the Lecturer/Senior Lecturer role, will have advanced academic qualifications and currently research active in a related field. Further details | Pūroko We have permanent positions at both full-time and part-time hours available (up to 1.3 FTE) from 1 October 2026, based in Wellington. Hours of work are negotiable, and candidates are asked to indicate their preference in their cover letter. Appointments will be made at Lecturer, Senior Lecturer, Professional Practice Fellow, or Senior Professional Practice Fellow level, depending on the successful candidate's skills, experience and academic achievements. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for these positions. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information about this role please contact Associate Professor Kirsty Danielson, Head of Department via the email address below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including cover letter and CV), please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601012 will close on Wednesday, 16 September 2026. Applications may be reviewed as they are received and the University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Note: If this is a part-time position, the salary range for this role will be the pro-rata equivalent of the annual full-time salary range. Contact: Associate Professor Kirsty Danielson Position details: Lecturer/Senior Lecturer , Professional Practice Fellow and Senior Professional Practice Fellow Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Wellington Primary Location NZL-NI-Wellington Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Surgery and Critical Care Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601129": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies",
      "description": "Job Description - Systems Analyst/Developer - NZ Mortality Review Committee (2601129) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Systems Analyst/Developer - NZ Mortality Review Committee (2601129) Systems Analyst/Developer - NZ Mortality Review Committee - 2601129 __________ DIVISION OF HEALTH SCIENCES | TE WĀHANGA MATUA MĀTAU HAUORA NEW ZEALAND MORTALITY REVIEW DATA GROUP | TE RŌPŪ KOHI PITOPITO MŌHIOTANGA MŌ TE TAKA MATE •    Competitive salary + 6.75% Superannuation + 5 Weeks Annual Leave. •    Work within a team grounded in ngākau pono (integrity) and mutual respect. •    We encourage continuous learning, professional growth, and career progression. About our team | Mō te tīma The New Zealand Mortality Review Data Group (NZMRDG) sits within the Department of Paediatrics and Child Health within the Faculty of Medicine – Dunedin.  The NZMRDG supports New Zealand’s National Mortality Review Committee | He Mutunga Kore, providing the systems and data that enable meaningful analysis and reporting to improve health outcomes. This opportunity is to join a collaborative team working with large, complex health datasets and modern web technologies. The role | Te mahi We're looking for a talented Systems Analyst/Developer to join the Department of Paediatrics and Child Health, supporting the systems used by the New Zealand Mortality Review Committee (NZMRDG). The NZMRDG plays a vital role in collecting, analysing and reporting mortality data to support national mortality review committees and inform recommendations that help reduce preventable deaths across Aotearoa New Zealand. In this role, you'll develop, enhance and maintain the systems that underpin this important work. Working within an experienced development team, you'll contribute to the ongoing evolution of web-based applications and databases while collaborating closely with researchers, analysts and subject matter experts. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako You'll be an enthusiastic software developer who enjoys solving problems and building practical solutions that make a difference.  To be successful, you'll have: •    A tertiary qualification in Computer Science, Information Science or a related discipline, or equivalent experience. •    Experience developing applications using PHP, ideally with the Laravel framework. •    Knowledge of relational databases such as MySQL. •    Experience with JavaScript and Python scripting. •    Strong analytical and problem-solving skills. •    Excellent written and interpersonal communication skills. •    The ability to translate user requirements into effective technical solutions. •    Strong organisational skills with the ability to manage competing priorities and work both independently and collaboratively. Further details | Pūroko This is a full-time position (37.5 hours per week), permanent position, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The appointment range for this role is $75,631 to $80,503. Top of range $88,750 per year. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people, and behaviours. This means that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills, and perspectives are valued and respected. For further information or to discuss the roles in confidence, please contact Dr Rachael Lawrence Lodge via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601129 will close on Sunday, 6 September 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Dr Rachael Lawrence Lodge Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Level 6 (Range of Appointments $75,631 to $80,503, Top of Range $88,750) Organisation Paediatrics and Child Health Job Function Business and Systems Analyst ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601152": {
      "title": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Politics of the Middle East (2601152)",
      "description": "Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Politics of the Middle East (2601152) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Politics of the Middle East (2601152) Lecturer/Senior Lecturer - Politics of the Middle East - 2601152 __________ THE POLITICS PROGRAMME | TŌRAKAPŪ DIVISION OF HUMANITIES | TE KETE ARONUI About us | Mō mātou The University of Otago is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities, ranked in the top 1% in the QS World University Rankings. Our Politics Programme was established in 1967 and has a well-established record of teaching and research. We offer a range of research-informed undergraduate and postgraduate courses in comparative politics/area studies, Aotearoa New Zealand politics, international relations, and political theory. We have a strong record of working in both teaching and research with scholars and programmes in the Division of Humanities and from across the university. The Politics Programme has a long-standing commitment to teaching and researching Politics of the Middle East, having for over twenty-five years offered a range of courses at undergraduate and postgraduate levels and supervised a significant number of research students in this area. The Programme was instrumental in establishing MEISA (Middle East and Islamic Studies Aotearoa), an interdisciplinary academic network that promotes research on the Middle East and Islamic worlds from a New Zealand perspective. The role | Te mahi Applications are invited for a Lecturer/Senior Lecturer position (Politics of the Middle East) in the Politics Programme. The position offers an academic career in a well-established programme with a proud teaching and research record. We are looking for a candidate with a demonstrated record of producing engaging, innovative teaching and research. Our ideal candidate will contribute to existing courses and develop new courses in Politics of the Middle East and Comparative Politics. The successful candidate will teach lower-level undergraduate courses on Politics of the Middle East and Comparative Politics, develop a 3rd year course in their area of research expertise, and either contribute to existing courses at Master’s level or develop their own course in their area of research expertise. They will be expected to: •    supervise research at Master’s and PhD levels; •    develop and maintain a strong research profile, including showing a willingness to seek external research funds; and •    undertake administrative roles and community/professional service activities that contribute to the overall effectiveness and standing of the Programme. Skills and Experience | Kā pūkeke me kā wheako The successful candidate is expected to have a PhD in Politics or related discipline and to be able to demonstrate a record of high-quality research, teaching, and service. The successful candidate will also demonstrate: •    A commitment to being a productive collaborator with a track record of working collegially. •    An excellent research profile along with a commitment to and/or established connections with international and/or national researchers. •    A record of delivering high-quality tertiary-level teaching. •    Ability in teaching Politics of the Middle East and Comparative Politics. •    A working knowledge of at least one Middle Eastern language. •    A commitment to deliver their own specialist courses to our programme. •    A commitment to contribute to team-taught courses. •    A commitment to undertake media and community outreach and engagement. •    Experience working with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds. Early career staff have a managed workload programme to enable them to develop their research activities through the first two years or so of their appointment. There will also be opportunities to complete a number of professional development courses related to the Treaty of Waitangi, te reo Māori, te aō Māori, and engaging with the University’s Māori and Pacific Strategic Frameworks. Further Information | Pūroko This is a permanent, full-time (1 FTE) position, based in Ōtepoti Dunedin, and is available from February 2027. The appointment ranges for this role are: •    Lecturer - $97,377 to $ 114,943 per annum. •    Senior Lecturer - $ 120,494 to $137,444 per annum. Appointment level will be determined by the candidate’s academic achievements, skills, and experience. Specific enquiries may be directed to Associate Professor James Headley, Head of Politics Programme, via the email address below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    A CV, including employment history. •    A letter of application outlining how you fit the role. •    Names and addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button below. Applications quoting reference number 2601152 will close on 11:59pm (NZST) on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Associate Professor James Headley Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow ($97,377 to $114,943) or Senior Lecturer / Senior Research Fellow ($120,494 to $137,444) Organisation Politics Job Function Research and Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601160": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies",
      "description": "Job Description - Occupational Health Nurse (2601160) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Occupational Health Nurse (2601160) Occupational Health Nurse - 2601160 __________ OPERATIONS DIVISION | TE WĀHANGA WHAKAHAERE HEALTH AND SAFETY COMPLIANCE | TE TIRA WHAIORA TAUTUKUNGA About us | Mō mātou At the University of Otago, we're committed to creating a safe, healthy, and thriving environment for our staff, students, and visitors. Our Health, Safety & Wellbeing team partners across the University to promote a culture where people can do their best work, stay well, and feel supported. We provide expert guidance, practical solutions, and proactive programmes that protect and enhance both physical and mental wellbeing. The role | Te mahi Join our Occupational Health team and contribute to the health, safety and wellbeing of university staff across a diverse and complex workplace. This is a varied role combining clinical nursing practice with prevention, health monitoring, rehabilitation and workplace health advice. Working as part of a collaborative multidisciplinary team, you will contribute to a range of occupational health services and initiatives, including: •    Occupational health assessments, health monitoring and workplace exposure programmes. •    Rehabilitation, stay-at-work and return-to-work support following illness or injury. •    Vaccination and immunisation programmes. •    Clinical assessment, advice and guidance to staff and leaders. •    Identification and management of occupational health risks. •    Health-related investigations and health promotion initiatives. •    Contributing to the ongoing development of occupational health services across the University. You will work with staff, leaders and colleagues across the University, using your clinical expertise and professional judgement to support healthy and sustainable participation in work. Skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We are looking for a Registered Nurse with a current practising certificate who is interested in occupational health and workplace wellbeing. You will bring: •    A commitment to culturally safe practice and a willingness to develop capability in Te Ao Māori, Te Tiriti o Waitangi principles and Pasifika values. •    Sound clinical judgement and the ability to apply nursing knowledge within a workplace setting. •    Vaccinator authorisation, or a willingness to obtain this qualification with support provided. •    Knowledge of rehabilitation, injury management and/or occupational health monitoring programmes is desirable. •    Knowledge of Health and Safety legislation and occupational health practices. •    Experience in occupational health is desirable. We also welcome applications from nurses with relevant experience in areas such as mental health, public health or practice nursing who are interested in developing their occupational health practice. •    Experience working effectively within multidisciplinary teams and across different professional groups. •    Strong communication and relationship-building skills, with the ability to provide clear clinical advice to staff and leaders. We will support your ongoing professional development and provide training as required to maintain and develop your occupational health nursing practice. We value diversity and recognise the contribution that different backgrounds, perspectives and experiences bring to our team. We encourage applications from people who meet the essential requirements and are interested in developing their career in occupational health. Further details | Pūroko This is a full time (37.5 hours), permanent position based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. While this role includes some on-site clinical responsibilities, we will consider flexible working options where operationally appropriate, including adjusted start and finish times and other arrangements that support both individual and team success. The appointment range for this role is $85,996 to $113,818 depending on qualifications and experience of the successful candidate. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. For further information, please contact (between the hours 9am to 4pm, Monday to Friday) Natasha Robinson, Health Clinic Coordinator Occupational Health via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601160 will close on Sunday, 6 September 2026 Applications may be reviewed as they are received. The university reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Natasha Robinson Tel: 0212790476 Position details: Job Description Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Please see information above Organisation Health and Safety Compliance Job Function Nursing and Midwifery ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601192": {
      "title": "Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry (2601192)",
      "description": "Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry (2601192) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry (2601192) Postdoctoral Fellow - Chemistry - 2601192 __________ DIVISION OF SCIENCES | TE ROHE A AHIKĀROA DEPARTMENT OF CHEMISTRY | TE TARI MĀTAURANGA MATŪ Mō te tīma | About our team The University of Otago, established in 1869, has a long and distinguished tradition of teaching and research. The Department of Chemistry at the University of Otago is the oldest chemistry department in New Zealand. It has grown to be one of the largest Departments in the Division of Sciences and contributes to various courses and programmes in Sciences and Health Sciences. Responsibility for the Department's dual mandate of teaching and research is shared among full-time academic staff and a number of teaching and research fellows, assisted by a similar number of technical, secretarial and general staff. As such, the Department is one of the largest and most active in New Zealand, with working conditions and facilities that rival those of many overseas departments. The role | Te mahi The Royal Society of New Zealand Marsden Fund has awarded the Ennis group a research grant to investigate the formation and structure of mixed clathrate hydrate minerals relevant to Titan’s surface; including the study of possible astrochemical pathways toward higher-order organic molecules. This postdoctoral fellow position is to support and advance all objectives of this laboratory astrochemistry research project, including the coordination of overseas experiments with expert collaborators at ANSTO Melbourne and Sydney, NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory California USA and the University of Western Australia. Key tasks of the role include: •    Contributing to the design and development of new laboratory capabilities including gas delivery manifolds, thin film deposition and thickness verification, vacuum hardware, photolysis setups. •    Carrying out the optimisation and maintenance of sample synthesis equipment, high vacuum equipment, gas handling systems and cryocooler hardware. •    Synthesis, purification and handling hazardous gaseous samples including astronomical nitriles. •    Performing data analysis including vibrational spectroscopy, mass spectrometry, X-ray and neutron diffraction crystallography. Perform supporting computations such as DFT calculations. •    Contributing toward novel experiment design. •    Liaising with international team to coordinate remote experiments and write beamline proposals. •    Assisting with the writing of scientific papers and journal articles. Kā pūkeka me kā wheako | Your skills and experience Successful candidates will have: •    A PhD in a relevant field (such as chemistry, physics, planetary science, or materials science). •    Previous experience with synthesis and purification of gas samples, including knowledge of safe handling and disposal procedures. •    Previous experience with laboratory high vacuum and cryogenic systems as applied to astrochemistry or surface science studies. •    Previous experience with powder x-ray and/or neutron diffraction and associated data analysis routines not essential, but advantageous. Pūroko | Further details This is a full-time (1.0 FTE), fixed-term position (for 2 years), available from 1 March 2027, based in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. The salary for this position is $92,350 per annum. Applications from candidates with Māori and Pacific backgrounds or strong competencies in these areas are warmly encouraged. The Dunedin campus is consistently ranked among the most beautiful in the world. The city offers affordable living, a safe environment, and everything within walking distance. Spectacular outdoor activities await right at the doorstep. For further information, please contact Dr Courtney Ennis, via the contact details below. Tono | Application Candidates are requested to submit: •    Cover letter •    Curriculum Vitae, including a list of publications •    Names and contact details of three referees To submit your application, please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601192 will close at 11:59 PM (NZST) on Sunday, 20 September 2026. Additional Information Contact: Dr Courtney Ennis Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Postdoctoral Fellow ($92,350) Organisation Chemistry Job Function Research ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2601163": {
      "title": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Clinical Trials Unit (Wellington) (2601163)",
      "description": "Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Clinical Trials Unit (Wellington) (2601163) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Teaching Fellow - Clinical Trials Unit (Wellington) (2601163) Teaching Fellow - Clinical Trials Unit (Wellington) - 2601163 __________ FACULTY OF MEDICINE | TE KAUPEKA WHAIORA UNIVERSITY OF OTAGO WELLINGTON | ŌTĀKOU WHAKAIHU WAKA, PŌNEKE About our team | Mō te tīma We are seeking a Teaching Fellow to join the Faculty of Medicine at University of Otago, Wellington and also under a separate contract support the Clinical Trials Unit (CTU) at Te Whatu Ora Capital and Coast and Hutt Valley. The role | Te mahi This is an exciting opportunity for a resident medical officer (with MCNZ General Registration at time of appointment) to gain broad exposure to academic medicine through a unique shared appointment between Te Whatu Ora – Capital, Coast and Hutt Valley and the Faculty of Medicine, University of Otago, Wellington. The role combines clinical trial support and medical student teaching. Key responsibilities include: •    Support ongoing clinical trials, including protocol familiarisation, data collection, patient engagement, and trial coordination tasks. •    Contribute to ethics applications, data management, and publication drafts. •    Assist in delivery of clinical teaching in MBChB modules and contribute to assessments and OSCEs. •    Participate in CTU meetings and assist with audit and quality assurance activities. •    Build collaborative relationships with clinician-researchers and partner institutions. Your Skills and Experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako •    Registered medical practitioner in New Zealand (with MCNZ General Registration at time of appointment). •    Demonstrated interest in clinical research and medical education. •    Strong organisational and communication skills. •    Ability to work independently and as part of a team. •    Self-directed and motivated individual, proactive in identifying and pursuing opportunities •    Understanding of Te Tiriti o Waitangi and commitment to its principles. •    Desirable: prior experience in clinical trials or teaching. Further details | Pūroko This is a combined fixed-term, full-time (1.0 FTE) position for 12 months, located in Pōneke, Wellington.  A 0.8 FTE position is negotiable for the right candidate. The position will be based in Wellington Regional Hospital, Hutt Hospital, and University of Otago, Wellington campus. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this position. The salary range for the academic position is $98,583 to $120,528 per annum (pro rata) - the level of appointment will be based on skills and experience. The University of Otago is a workplace that values and utilises diverse and inclusive thinking, people and behaviours. This means that we honour Te Tiriti o Waitangi and that the contributions of staff with diverse backgrounds, experiences, skills and perspectives are valued and respected. For more information regarding the role, please contact Professor Rebecca Grainger via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2601163 will close on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received and suitable candidates may be interviewed. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Professor Rebecca Grainger Position details: Teaching Fellow & Research Fellow Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Wellington Primary Location NZL-NI-Wellington Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range Lecturer / Research Fellow - Medical & Dental ($98,583 to $120,528) Organisation Medicine Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2600967": {
      "title": "Current Vacancies",
      "description": "Job Description - Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) (2600967) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) (2600967) Graduate Dentist Intern (Dunedin/Auckland) - 2600967 __________ CLINICAL SERVICES FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO Are you a new or recent dental graduate seeking an exciting opportunity to join a supportive culture founded on teamwork, integrity, and social responsibility? We are looking for passionate and driven graduate dentists to join our modern team based dental practice at our facilities in Ōtepoti, Dunedin and Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland. The role | Te mahi The Faculty of Dentistry invites applications for up to four full-time fixed term positions across both campuses with the possibility of three positions in Ōtepoti, Dunedin and one position in Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland, commencing 2027. In these roles you will work with experienced clinicians to provide a wide scope of general dental procedures including urgent care. Throughout this placement, you can benefit from ongoing mentoring, training, support, and coaching. These early career positions offer a unique opportunity where you can have the ability to gain valuable clinical experience under the mentorship of experienced dentists and academics within the Faculty of Dentistry, University of Otago. You can build and enhance your clinical skills in a culturally supportive environment that includes dental treatment delivering planned treatments and urgent care. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako We seek Dentists within their first three years of working, who could value interacting with other professionals and who thrive on working in a supportive environment founded on teamwork, integrity, and social responsibility. You aspire to form a firm foundation of clinical skills and there can be opportunities to get involved in teaching, research, or postgraduate learning. To be successful in this role, you should also have: •    Strong knowledge and understanding in all areas of general dentistry. •    Exceptional communication and patient care skills. •    An ability to work in a large cohesive team environment. •    Outstanding time management and organisational skills. •    A strong desire to consolidate your clinical skills in a supportive environment. •    An ability to provide high quality dental care to all age groups of patients. •    A drive to support students as a mentor in areas commensurate with your clinical expertise. •    Knowledge or the ability to acquire knowledge of procedures, policies, regulations and standards of practice. Applicants must be registered or registrable with the Dental Council of New Zealand (DCNZ). Further details | Puroko We have multiple, full-time (1 FTE), fixed term (18 January 2027 – 14 January 2028) positions, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin or Tāmaki Makaurau, Auckland. The salary for these positions is $79,812 per annum. You must have the right to live and work in New Zealand to apply for this job. For further information, please contact Yolin Govender (Auckland) or Pip MacDonald (Dunedin) via the contact details below. Application | Tono To submit your application (including CV and cover letter) please click the apply button. Applications quoting reference number 2600967 will close on Sunday, 13 September 2026. Applications may be considered as they are received. The University reserves the right to close this vacancy at any time. Additional Information Contact: Yolin Govender (Auckland) Contact: Pip MacDonald (Dunedin) Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Fixed Term Salary Level and Range The University is offering a remuneration level commensurate with the responsibilities of the position. Organisation Clinical Services Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    },
    "https://otago.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=2501589": {
      "title": "Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry (2501589)",
      "description": "Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry (2501589) Go to the main content section. Human Resources Information for Staff Working @ Otago Join Otago About Otago University Application toolkit Living & working in New Zealand Career development & training Health & Safety HR toolkit HR policies Contacts & enquiries Current Vacancies Otago staff should use the internal version Welcome. You are not signed in. | My Account Options My Jobs | Sign In This service is set to disconnect automatically after {0} minutes of inactivity. Your session will end in {1} minutes. Click OK to reset the timer to {0} minutes. You have been signed out. This service is set to sign out after {0} minutes of inactivity. Job Search My Job Page Basic Search | Advanced Search | Jobs Matching My Profile | All Jobs Beginning of the main content section. Return to the home page Printable Format Job Description - Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry (2501589) Senior Professional Practice Fellow - Dentistry - 2501589 __________ DIVISION OF HEALTH SCIENCES | TE WĀHANGA MATUA MĀTAU HAUORA FACULTY OF DENTISTRY | TE KAUPEKA PŪNIHO Who we are | Ko wai mātou The University of Otago is one of New Zealand’s largest and most research-intensive universities. Founded in 1907, the Faculty of Dentistry is based at the University’s main campus in Dunedin with clinics in Auckland and South Dunedin and is the only dental school in New Zealand. We are proud of our international reputation for research, clinical, and academic excellence. The 2023 QS World University Subject Rankings list the Dental School as 42nd in the world. The Faculty of Dentistry comprises of three Departments (Oral Rehabilitation, Oral Diagnostic and Surgical and Oral Sciences) as well as the Sir John Walsh Research Institute. Dunedin offers an outstanding quality of life, combining the vibrancy of a university city with the warmth of a close-knit community. The region provides exceptional schooling and educational opportunities, making it an ideal place for families. Dunedin is perfectly placed for outdoor pursuits, with world-class mountain bike trails, nearby ski fields, and excellent surfing. Just a few hours from Queenstown—the adventure capital of New Zealand—it also offers easy access to some of the country’s most stunning natural landscapes. Dunedin’s wildlife is world-renowned, home to the royal albatross, seals, penguins, and an array of native birds. ‘The outdoors and wildlife was one of our biggest pulls to this area as a family coming from the UK - also the schools are fantastic!’  H. Smith, Senior Professional Practice Fellow. The role | Te mahi The University is seeking applications from suitably qualified candidates for the position of Senior Professional Practice Fellows in Dentistry. As a Senior Professional Practice Fellow, you will help support teaching, facilitate learning, assist with assessments, and carry out clinical service/clinical activity both on undergraduate clinics and staff clinics out of term time. Some key tasks of the role include: •    Clinical teaching/tutoring of undergraduate dentists. •    Contributing to the Department’s academic goals by applying expert clinical knowledge in your area of specialty. •    Preparation, assessment, teaching and paper administration duties. •    Following through patient care and referrals within the hospital. Your skills and experience | Kā pūkeka me kā wheako Successful applicants: •    Must hold a dental degree and have registration or be eligible for full registration with the Dental Council of New Zealand. •    Will demonstrate the ability to work well with students and staff from differing academic and cultural backgrounds and at all levels within the institution. •    Show a commitment to uphold the principles of the Treaty of Waitangi and the University’s Māori Strategic Framework. Candidates will also have and maintain registration (if supervised) including: •    A practicing certificate in place before starting work. •    Indemnity in place prior to starting work. Further details | Pūroko We have eight job openings, offering both permanent and fixed-term positions, located in Ōtepoti, Dunedin. We have opportunities for Dentists with special interests in Endodontics, Prosthodontics and Periodontics. Both full-time (1 FTE) and part-time positions are available, allowing for employment over the full year or semester time only. The duration of any fixed-term positions can be negotiable with the successful candidates. International candidates would need to be available to work minimum 0.8FTE. The salary range for these positions is $109,554 to $184,993 per annum (pro rata). Candidates will be appointed to a step on the Senior Professional Practice Fellow scale 3 or 4, dependent on qualifications and skills. The University is committed to meeting its obligations under the Children’s Act 2014.  Candidates who successfully make the final stages of the recruitment process will be required to undergo a safety check. Specific enquiries about this position may be directed to Dr Pip MacDonald via the contact details below. Application | Tono Candidates are requested to submit the following: •    CV. •    Cover letter. •    Names and email addresses of at least three referees. To submit your application, please click the apply button. Applications should quote reference number 2501598. There is no formal closing date for this role. We may begin interviewing suitable candidates at any  time during the recruitment process and reserve the right to make an appointment before the advertised closing date. LI-DNI Additional Information Contact: Dr Pip MacDonald Position details: Information Statement Guidelines for Academic Positions: Guidelines Further Information: Department Website & Faculty of Dentistry Video Tour Create an email with a link to this vacancy: Create email Location: About Dunedin Primary Location NZL-SI-Dunedin Employment Status Permanent Salary Level and Range Senior Teaching & Professional Practice Fellow - Medical & Dental 3 ($109,554 to $120,528) or 4 ($148,414 to $184,993) Organisation Office of the Head of Faculty (Dentistry) Job Function Teaching ^ Top of page | Accessibility | Copyright & Disclaimer | Contact Us | Feedback | Top of page © Human Resources 1st Floor Registry Building PO Box 56 Dunedin 9054 New Zealand Tel 64 3 479 8269 Fax 64 3 479 8279 Email human.resources@otago.ac.nz"
    }
  },
